# hilavitkutin-frozen-authors Design Overview

`#![no_std]`, no alloc, no `unsafe`, no feature gate, `publish = false`. Work
units written the way a consumer writes them, each beside a hand model of what
it computes, and kept frozen. It depends on `hilavitkutin-api`, on
`hilavitkutin` for the context type a unit names, and on `arvo`,
`arvo-tensor` and `notko`.

## Purpose

The contract a unit author writes against is the thing the engine promises to
keep. Planning, fusion, threading and every later optimisation are meant to
happen behind it, with no edit asked of an author. A promise like that holds
only as long as something notices when it is broken, and a test suite does not:
when a contract change breaks a test, the test gets edited in the same commit
and nobody counts it.

So these units are frozen. They are written once, as a consumer would write
them, and from then on a file of this crate changes only together with a
written record of what forced the change. The count of those records is the
measure of how well the contract has held.

Beside the units sits a hand model: the same frame, spelled out over plain
arrays with no work unit, no access set and no store in it. The model is what
the reference runner of `hilavitkutin-conformance` is held to, and the
reference runner is what the scheduler is held to, so a defect the units and
the api share cannot hide in both.

## The plane and its stores

The records are a plane of `WIDE` by `TALL` places laid out one line after the
other, so a step east is one record on and a step north is `WIDE` records on.
`RECORDS` is the count of them.

Every value is a newtype over `Num`, an unsigned 32-bit number.

| Store | Kind | Holds |
|---|---|---|
| `Column<Pos>` | plain | A position, moved in place every frame. |
| `Column<Vel>` | plain | A velocity, never written. |
| `Field` | `Buffered<Heat, Exchange>` | The heat of each place. |
| `Column<Edge>` | plain | Whether a place lies on the west or the east side, never written. |
| `Accum<Crossed>` | accumulator | One value for each position that crossed `THRESHOLD` in the frame. |
| `Virtual<Overheat>` | virtual | Fired in a frame that began with a place at or over `LIMIT`. |
| `Column<Alarms>` | plain | How many frames found the plane overheated. |
| `Pool` | `Buffered<Level, Snapshot>` | A level that spreads along the line until it is even. |
| `Column<Peak>` | plain | The highest level a record has held. |
| `Column<Glow>` | plain | Twice the position. |
| `Column<Ash>` | plain | The glow plus `LIMIT`. |

## The units

Nine units, listed here in the sequence they are registered and run. Each is
a unit struct with the three impls the api asks of a value-form unit and
nothing else: `BuilderInput`, `WorkUnit` with `execute` as the one line the
api documents, and `RecordOp` with the function from the values read to the
values written.

| Unit | Schedule | Reads | Writes | Computes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `Drift` | always | `Column<Pos>`, `Column<Vel>` | `Column<Pos>` | The position plus the velocity. |
| `Spread` | always | `Field` with a reach of `WIDE`, `Column<Edge>` | `Field` | The heat of a place plus that of its four neighbours. |
| `Cross` | always | `Column<Pos>`, `Column<Vel>` | `Accum<Crossed>` | An append where the position crossed `THRESHOLD` in this frame. |
| `Alarm` | always | `Field` | `Virtual<Overheat>` | Fires where the heat is at or over `LIMIT`. |
| `Tally` | on `Overheat` | `Column<Alarms>` | `Column<Alarms>` under `Written` | The count plus one. |
| `Smooth` | always | `Pool` with a reach of `ONE` | `Pool` under `Differs` | The highest of a record's level and its two neighbours'. |
| `Crest` | always | `Pool`, `Column<Peak>` | `Column<Peak>` under `Differs` | The higher of the peak and the level. |
| `Warm` | always | `Column<Pos>` | `Column<Glow>` | Twice the position. |
| `Fade` | always | `Column<Glow>` | `Column<Ash>` | The glow plus `LIMIT`. |

`Spread` is the neighbour reader on two axes. Its east and west neighbours are
one record away and its north and south neighbours `WIDE` records away, all
through one `Window`. A neighbour past either end of the records reads as
nothing, and the `Edge` column says where a step east or west would leave the
plane's side and wrap onto the next line.

Between them the units emit every kind of trigger. `Cross` appends to an
accumulator. `Alarm` fires a virtual that `Tally` is scheduled on. `Tally`
marks its column changed whenever it runs, and `Smooth` and `Crest` mark
theirs only where the value differs, so the `Pool` goes quiet once it is even
and a scheduler may rest what reads it.

`Warm` and `Fade` are a chain: each reads one plain column and writes the
next, so the two of them alone are a carrier the scheduler can fuse into one
loop, and the fused entry is held to the same model as the others.

The sequence matters in three places. `Cross` and `Warm` are listed behind
`Drift` and read the position `Drift` wrote in the same frame. `Fade` reads
what `Warm` wrote. `Alarm` is listed behind `Spread` and still reads the heat
the frame began with, because the `Field` is buffered.

## The model

`Model` holds the frame's state as plain arrays of `RECORDS` values and steps
it with `frame`. `Model::new` is the starting state every run begins from: the
runner's lent storage and the scheduler's columns are filled from it.

`record::words` writes one frame's state down as words in a fixed sequence:
the positions, the heat, the alarm counts, the levels, the peaks, the glow,
the ash, whether `Overheat` fired, the count of crossed values and then the
values. It takes
the frame as one `record::State`, which holds the stores as slices and the
flag in that sequence, so the same function records the model, the reference
runner and the scheduler, and a comparison between any two of them is a
comparison of what it wrote.

## The ledger

`LEDGER.toml` has one entry for each frozen file of the crate: its path, what
kind of file it is, the hash of its content, how many times it has been broken
and whether it is locked. `BREAKS.toml` has one entry for each time a frozen
file had to change: the file, its new hash and the reason, in a sentence that
names what in the contract forced it.

A lint of the repository holds the two to the tree. It fails when a file's
content does not match its hash, when a file of the crate has no entry, when a
hash changed without a break recorded for it, and when a locked file changed at
all. Two more lints keep the crate written as a consumer could write it: no
feature gate and no raised recursion limit. A fourth keeps every file the
crate compiles under the ledger: a frozen file carries no `path` attribute
and none of the `include!` macros, since either brings in a file from
wherever it points, the unfrozen `tests/` included, and the ledger has no
entry for it.

The files under `tests/` are not frozen. They are the harness that runs the
units, and they change with the instruments.
